+++ b/domains/findings/pages/naming-a-reach-would-empty-the-import-index.finding.ts
@@ -8,5 +8,5 @@ export const namingAReachWouldEmptyTheImportIndex = {
   claim:
     "This was true when filed at 20:43 and was answered within the hour. The index filed an edge only for a specifier opening with a dot, so rewriting the akasha imports that reach past a package edge to the names their manifests state would have blinded the required reading gate, the folder shape check and the repointing a move does. `347d2776cc` gave `landingOf` a naming and `f62007f1b6` made the index file an edge for a package specifier; the rewrite followed at 22:14 and 22:23.",
   evidence:
-    "What it described: `landingOf` in `code-specifier` answered null for any specifier not matching `/^\\.\\.?\\//`, `edgesIn` in `index-import` dropped a null landing, and the index page said outright `A relative specifier makes an edge and a package specifier makes none.`\n\nNone of those three sentences holds now. `code-specifier.module.code.ts:58-65` takes a `naming` and answers `naming.get(specifier) ?? null` for a specifier that is not relative. `index-import.index.code.ts:19-31` takes that naming and hands it to `landingOf` at :26. The index page now states instead: `A relative specifier makes an edge.` beside `A specifier naming a package makes an edge at the file the naming handed in resolves it to.` and `A specifier the naming handed in does not resolve makes none.`\n\nProved against the standing index rather than the source: the import index entry for `code-specifier.module.code.ts` carries thirteen importer lines, twelve of which reach it by the bare specifier `@akasha/code-system/code-specifier`. The index really does carry `@akasha/...` edges.\n\nOne number here was already stale as written and is worth correcting for anyone reading the history: eighteen akasha packages stood in the root workspaces list, and twenty-five stand there now.\n\nWhat this does not cover is the one reader that was left unthreaded. `no-import-cycle` still calls `landingOf` with no naming, so a cycle running through a package edge lands unrefused, and the cost of that is no longer nothing now the rewrite has landed. That is carried by a-cycle-through-a-package-edge-goes-unseen.",
+    "What it described: `landingOf` in `code-specifier` answered null for any specifier not matching `/^\\.\\.?\\//`, `edgesIn` in `index-import` dropped a null landing, and the index page said outright `A relative specifier makes an edge and a package specifier makes none.`\n\nNone of those three sentences holds now. `code-specifier.module.code.ts:58-65` takes a `naming` and answers `naming.get(specifier) ?? null` for a specifier that is not relative. `index-import.index.code.ts:19-31` takes that naming and hands it to `landingOf` at :26. The index page now states instead: `A relative specifier makes an edge.` beside `A specifier naming a package makes an edge at the file the naming handed in resolves it to.` and `A specifier the naming handed in does not resolve makes none.`\n\nProved against the standing index rather than the source: the import index entry for `code-specifier.module.code.ts` carries thirteen importer lines, twelve of which reach it by the bare specifier `@akasha/code/code-specifier`. The index really does carry `@akasha/...` edges.\n\nOne number here was already stale as written and is worth correcting for anyone reading the history: eighteen akasha packages stood in the root workspaces list, and twenty-five stand there now.\n\nWhat this does not cover is the one reader that was left unthreaded. `no-import-cycle` still calls `landingOf` with no naming, so a cycle running through a package edge lands unrefused, and the cost of that is no longer nothing now the rewrite has landed. That is carried by a-cycle-through-a-package-edge-goes-unseen.",
 } as const satisfies Finding
